id: E2182
draft: true
title: HMDFS write-access predicates do not prove task-root directory creation
severity: 2
keywords: HMDFS, hishell_hap, mkdir, Operation not permitted, test -w, remote-root, W3, launch-count, successor
symptom: A W3 preflight reports WRITE_ACCESS=yes for an HMDFS parent, yet the first mkdir of the exact task root returns Operation not permitted. Retrying mkdir -p or changing umask does not create the root, and no transfer or runner launch occurs.
root_cause: The shell -w predicate and POSIX mode bits only describe discretionary access metadata. They do not prove that the HMDFS and hishell_hap policy will authorize child-directory creation at that exact path, so parent writability cannot be promoted to task-root readiness.
fix: Treat exact create-new task-root creation as its own transaction phase after inventory, fingerprint, boot and UID checks. If mkdir returns a clear failure, use the frozen status entry to prove the root absent, record W3=NOT_RUN and launch_count=0, and create a new run/freeze at a task-owned parent whose exact root creation succeeds. Preserve shared-group traversal with umask 007; never replay the failed run.
verification: Two GLM successors targeting currentUser/Download returned EPERM at root creation even though the parent was mode 2771 and test -w returned yes; both remained absent with launch_count=0. A successor rooted directly under currentUser created its root and transferred the frozen payload. The final independent r005 then completed exact-byte transfer, native self-sign, one launch, receipt chain 10/10, state 4/4, consumer 1/1, release and postrelease absence.
scope: OpenHarmony W3 controllers that create task-private directories on HMDFS from a hishell_hap SSH session, especially when choosing between writable-looking user subdirectories.
safety_boundary: Do not use test -w, mode bits, connectivity or a different task's existing directory as creation proof. Do not chmod shared parents, scan or reuse foreign roots, retry a failed run, or combine evidence across run IDs; a failed root phase stays NOT_RUN even when a successor later passes.
contributor_id: A7F3
